#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""prometheus-phrasebank — the offline phrase bank (Phase 2, tasks 2.8-2.9).

  prometheus-phrasebank generate [--events a,b] [--variants N] [--if-stale]
  prometheus-phrasebank pick EVENT [--slot k=v] [--count N] [--dry]
  prometheus-phrasebank show [EVENT]
  prometheus-phrasebank add EVENT "text"      write one of your own
  prometheus-phrasebank veto EVENT "text"     cut one, permanently
  prometheus-phrasebank verify [--prune]
  prometheus-phrasebank bench [-n N]
  prometheus-phrasebank status

ADR-0004 in one sentence: separate *when the words are written* from *when they
are spoken*. `generate` is the authoring pass — a large local model, persona.md,
about-me.md and phrasebank-spec.json in, as many validated phrasings per moment
as that moment honestly carries out, and nobody waiting on any of it.

`pick` is the runtime path — a dictionary lookup and a weighted random choice,
measured at 9-20 us, with no model loaded and no network involved. Nothing in
`pick` may ever touch Ollama.

The validator is the component. Small models write the same sentence forty times
with one word swapped, and they reach for status-report vocabulary ("active",
"ready", "loaded") the moment they run out of ideas — so every candidate is
checked for the word ceiling, the required placeholders, banned vocabulary,
punctuation that cannot be heard, and near-duplication against everything
already accepted. Rejections are the normal case, not the exception: expect to
throw away half of what the model writes.

Every candidate, accepted or rejected, is logged with its reason to
~/.local/state/prometheus/phrasebank.log. That log is the evidence for whether
the authoring model is earning its place.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import atexit
import base64
import difflib
import hashlib
import json
import os
import random
import re
import socket
import subprocess
import sys
import threading
import time
import urllib.error
import urllib.request

HOME = os.path.expanduser("~")
REPO_DIR = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)))
